    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d586">

                <pb facs="0561" n="549" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg549"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/2043: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Thurston</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow via Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-04-09T18:00:00+03:00">April 9, 1940—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 10—7:05 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>362. My 361, April 9.<anchor n="91" xml:id="d586-f1"/> I am informed in strictest
                    confidence that Molotov this afternoon received the German Ambassador with great
                    cordiality and in regard of the German actions against Denmark and Norway<anchor
                        n="92" xml:id="d586-f2"/> told him that the Soviet Government fully
                    understood the “defensive character” of these measures.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Thurston</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d586-f1" n="91" xml:id="d586-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; in this telegram the Chargé in the Soviet Union reported that a
                    member of the German Embassy had stated that advance information of the German
                    action against Denmark and Norway had been brought to Moscow by special courier
                    late in the afternoon of April 8. Because Molotov “was in the country,” this
                    information could not be given to him before the following afternoon. It was
                    believed, however, that the Soviet Government had already been informed through
                    its Embassy in Berlin. (740.0011 European War 1939/2037)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d586-f2" n="92" xml:id="d586-f2-content">
                <p>For correspondence regarding the invasion of Norway and Denmark by Germany, see
                        <ref target="#pg136">pp. 136</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
